---
import ProjectLayout from "../../../layouts/ProjectLayout.astro";
---

<ProjectLayout
  title="Robotic Gripper"
  category="Project | Adaptive Gripping"
  year="2020"
  description="High-school engineering project focused on the design of a flexible robotic gripper capable of adapting to different object shapes and surface geometries."
>

  <p slot="intro">
    An adaptive robotic gripper developed during my Sciences de l’Ingénieur studies,
    using flexible gripping elements to conform to different object geometries
    and improve contact during grasping.
  </p>


  <!-- PROJECT SNAPSHOT -->

  <section class="portfolio-hero">

    <div class="portfolio-outcome">

      <span class="portfolio-kicker">
        Outcome
      </span>

      <h2>
        Gripping through compliance instead of complexity.
      </h2>

      <p>
        The project explored how flexible gripping elements could adapt mechanically
        to different object shapes, reducing the need for complex active adjustment.
      </p>

      <div class="portfolio-metrics">

        <div>
          <strong>Flexible</strong>
          <span>Adaptive gripping elements</span>
        </div>

        <div>
          <strong>Passive</strong>
          <span>Shape adaptation</span>
        </div>

        <div>
          <strong>Prototype</strong>
          <span>Designed and tested</span>
        </div>

      </div>

    </div>


    <figure class="portfolio-main-visual">

      <img
        src="/images/projects/robotic-gripper/gripper-main.jpg"
        alt="Flexible robotic gripper prototype"
      />

      <figcaption>
        Prototype | Adaptive flexible robotic gripper
      </figcaption>

    </figure>

  </section>


  <!-- 01 | CONTEXT -->

  <section class="detail-section">

    <div class="detail-index">
      01
    </div>

    <div class="detail-copy">

      <h2>
        Context
      </h2>

      <p>
        The project was developed during my Sciences de l’Ingénieur studies
        in high school.
      </p>

      <p>
        The objective was to design a robotic gripper capable of handling objects
        with different shapes and surface geometries.
      </p>

      <p>
        Instead of relying on rigid jaws designed for one specific geometry,
        the concept used flexible gripping elements capable of deforming around
        the object.
      </p>

    </div>

  </section>


  <!-- 02 | DESIGN CHALLENGE -->

  <section class="portfolio-visual-section">

    <div class="portfolio-section-header">

      <span>
        02 | Design challenge
      </span>

      <h2>
        One gripper. Many geometries.
      </h2>

    </div>


    <div class="process-grid">

      <figure>

        <img
          src="/images/projects/robotic-gripper/initial-sketch.jpg"
          alt="Initial robotic gripper concept sketch"
        />

        <figcaption>
          01 | Initial concept
        </figcaption>

      </figure>


      <figure>

        <img
          src="/images/projects/robotic-gripper/flexible-finger.jpg"
          alt="Flexible robotic gripper finger concept"
        />

        <figcaption>
          02 | Flexible gripping element
        </figcaption>

      </figure>


      <figure>

        <img
          src="/images/projects/robotic-gripper/prototype.jpg"
          alt="Physical robotic gripper prototype"
        />

        <figcaption>
          03 | Physical prototype
        </figcaption>

      </figure>

    </div>

  </section>


  <!-- 03 | OBJECTIVES -->

  <section class="detail-section">

    <div class="detail-index">
      03
    </div>

    <div class="detail-copy">

      <h2>
        Engineering objectives
      </h2>

      <ul>
        <li>
          Grip objects with different shapes
        </li>

        <li>
          Adapt to irregular surfaces
        </li>

        <li>
          Increase contact area during gripping
        </li>

        <li>
          Reduce the need for precise object positioning
        </li>

        <li>
          Limit mechanical complexity
        </li>

        <li>
          Create a functional physical prototype
        </li>
      </ul>

    </div>

  </section>


  <!-- 04 | MY CONTRIBUTION -->

  <section class="detail-section">

    <div class="detail-index">
      04
    </div>

    <div class="detail-copy">

      <h2>
        My contribution
      </h2>

      <ul>
        <li>
          Contributed to the gripping concept
        </li>

        <li>
          Worked on flexible finger geometry
        </li>

        <li>
          Developed mechanical components
        </li>

        <li>
          Participated in prototype assembly
        </li>

        <li>
          Evaluated gripping behaviour on different shapes
        </li>

        <li>
          Iterated the design based on physical testing
        </li>
      </ul>

    </div>

  </section>


  <!-- 05 | FLEXIBLE GRIPPING -->

  <section class="portfolio-system">

    <div class="portfolio-section-header">

      <span>
        05 | Flexible gripping
      </span>

      <h2>
        Letting the mechanism adapt to the object.
      </h2>

    </div>


    <div class="system-grid">

      <figure class="system-overview">

        <img
          src="/images/projects/robotic-gripper/system-overview.jpg"
          alt="Overall architecture of the flexible robotic gripper"
        />

        <figcaption>
          System architecture | Actuation and flexible fingers
        </figcaption>

      </figure>


      <div class="subsystem-stack">

        <figure>

          <img
            src="/images/projects/robotic-gripper/finger-detail.jpg"
            alt="Detailed flexible robotic gripper finger"
          />

          <figcaption>
            Finger detail | Flexible contact geometry
          </figcaption>

        </figure>


        <div class="subsystem-copy">

          <h3>
            Passive adaptation
          </h3>

          <p>
            The flexible fingers deform when they contact an object.
          </p>

          <p>
            This deformation allows the gripping surface to conform to the
            local geometry of the object and distribute contact over a larger area.
          </p>

        </div>

      </div>

    </div>

  </section>


  <!-- 06 | COMPLIANCE -->

  <section class="detail-section">

    <div class="detail-index">
      06
    </div>

    <div class="detail-copy">

      <h2>
        Mechanical compliance
      </h2>

      <p>
        Compliance was the central principle of the project.
      </p>

      <p>
        A rigid gripper must usually know the geometry and position of an object
        with relatively high accuracy.
      </p>

      <p>
        A flexible gripper can absorb part of this uncertainty mechanically,
        allowing the fingers themselves to adapt during contact.
      </p>

    </div>

  </section>


  <!-- 07 | CONTACT -->

  <section class="detail-section">

    <div class="detail-index">
      07
    </div>

    <div class="detail-copy">

      <h2>
        Contact behaviour
      </h2>

      <p>
        Increasing the contact area can improve the stability of a grasp,
        especially when handling objects with curved or irregular surfaces.
      </p>

      <p>
        The project therefore focused not only on closing the gripper,
        but on how the fingers interacted mechanically with the object.
      </p>

    </div>

  </section>


  <!-- MOTION VISUAL -->

  <section class="portfolio-wide-visual">

    <figure>

      <img
        src="/images/projects/robotic-gripper/gripping-study.jpg"
        alt="Flexible gripper adapting to multiple object shapes"
      />

      <figcaption>
        Gripping study | Adaptation to different object geometries
      </figcaption>

    </figure>

  </section>


  <!-- 08 | TESTING -->

  <section class="detail-section">

    <div class="detail-index">
      08
    </div>

    <div class="detail-copy">

      <h2>
        Physical testing
      </h2>

      <p>
        The prototype was used to observe how the flexible fingers behaved
        on objects with different geometries.
      </p>

      <p>
        Physical tests helped identify limitations that were difficult to evaluate
        from geometry alone, particularly local deformation and contact behaviour.
      </p>

    </div>

  </section>


  <!-- 09 | DEVELOPMENT -->

  <section class="portfolio-visual-section">

    <div class="portfolio-section-header">

      <span>
        09 | Development
      </span>

      <h2>
        Concept. Build. Grip. Refine.
      </h2>

    </div>


    <div class="process-grid">

      <figure>

        <img
          src="/images/projects/robotic-gripper/design.jpg"
          alt="Robotic gripper design phase"
        />

        <figcaption>
          Design
        </figcaption>

      </figure>


      <figure>

        <img
          src="/images/projects/robotic-gripper/build.jpg"
          alt="Robotic gripper prototype assembly"
        />

        <figcaption>
          Build
        </figcaption>

      </figure>


      <figure>

        <img
          src="/images/projects/robotic-gripper/test.jpg"
          alt="Robotic gripper testing"
        />

        <figcaption>
          Test
        </figcaption>

      </figure>

    </div>

  </section>


  <!-- 10 | DOCUMENTATION -->

  <section class="portfolio-documents">

    <div class="portfolio-section-header">

      <span>
        10 | Documentation
      </span>

      <h2>
        Project records.
      </h2>

    </div>


    <div class="document-grid">

      <a
        href="/documents/robotic-gripper/project-report.pdf"
        target="_blank"
        class="document-card"
      >

        <div>

          <span class="document-type">
            PDF
          </span>

          <h3>
            Project report
          </h3>

          <p>
            Design approach, prototype development and project conclusions.
          </p>

        </div>

        <span class="document-arrow">
          ↗
        </span>

      </a>


      <a
        href="/documents/robotic-gripper/design-study.pdf"
        target="_blank"
        class="document-card"
      >

        <div>

          <span class="document-type">
            PDF
          </span>

          <h3>
            Design study
          </h3>

          <p>
            Flexible finger concepts, mechanical choices and development iterations.
          </p>

        </div>

        <span class="document-arrow">
          ↗
        </span>

      </a>


      <a
        href="/documents/robotic-gripper/test-report.pdf"
        target="_blank"
        class="document-card"
      >

        <div>

          <span class="document-type">
            PDF
          </span>

          <h3>
            Test report
          </h3>

          <p>
            Prototype observations and gripping tests on different geometries.
          </p>

        </div>

        <span class="document-arrow">
          ↗
        </span>

      </a>

    </div>

  </section>


  <!-- 11 | SKILLS -->

  <section class="detail-section">

    <div class="detail-index">
      11
    </div>

    <div class="detail-copy">

      <h2>
        Engineering skills
      </h2>

      <ul>
        <li>
          Mechanical design
        </li>

        <li>
          Flexible mechanisms
        </li>

        <li>
          Adaptive gripping
        </li>

        <li>
          Rapid prototyping
        </li>

        <li>
          CAD modelling
        </li>

        <li>
          Physical testing
        </li>

        <li>
          Design iteration
        </li>
      </ul>

    </div>

  </section>


  <!-- 12 | LEARNING -->

  <section class="detail-section">

    <div class="detail-index">
      12
    </div>

    <div class="detail-copy">

      <h2>
        What I learned
      </h2>

      <p>
        This project introduced me to the idea that a mechanism does not always
        need additional sensors or actuators to become more adaptable.
      </p>

      <p>
        Mechanical flexibility itself can provide useful behaviour by allowing
        the structure to react naturally to contact with its environment.
      </p>

      <p>
        Looking back, this project was also an early introduction to ideas that
        later became central to my interest in soft and biomimetic robotics.
      </p>

    </div>

  </section>

</ProjectLayout>